From e69b99c66170e0395d52e106427429f4f9179f84 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Micheal Waltz <micheal@ecliptik.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 06:54:03 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] DOS-PORT: patch 0297 -- FindGUIID: remember successful finds
 until the GUI list is replaced

WHY: old verb-template scripts look GUIs up by name every tick. Maniac Mansion Deluxe's
repeatedly_execute calls FindGUIID 23 times a tick, and each call scans every GUI name with a
case-insensitive compare to return an answer that cannot change while the game runs
(LAB-MMD.md 12). Measured at fixed 40000 cycles on MMD's character select: the script event went
from 9.474 to 9.377 ms/tick (-0.097; the pre-registered band was -0.06 to -0.143). AGS Quest makes
no calls and does not change. The 486 prediction is -0.30 to -0.65 ms/tick. Its falsifying cell is
HW-MMD-5, and the per-call cost behind it is an upper bound because of an L1-cache confound
(LAB-MMD.md 13.2).

WHAT: a 16-entry direct-mapped cache in front of the original scan, now FindGUIIDScan. An entry is
keyed by the query pointer and confirmed against a copy of the query text, so a reused pointer
with different text misses and scans. Only successful finds are remembered, so a miss still quits
exactly as before. Queries of 32 bytes or more are not cached. FindGUIIDCacheReset() runs where the
GUI list is replaced: in unload_game after guis.clear(), and in InitGameState after
guis = std::move(ents.Guis).

Exact because GUI names are set only while game data loads. GUI::ReadGUI's renames act on the
loaded list before InitGameState moves it in. No script API renames a GUI.
GUIMain::ReadFromSavegame reads no name, and a restore asserts that the GUI count is unchanged.
Gated to __DJGPP__: the scan is only worth avoiding on a CPU where it shows up per tick.

Equivalence: state digests every 25 ticks, script events, AutoPilot log and PH*.BMP equal to the
baseline on MMD, AGS Quest and 5 Days (seeded), with baseline-twice and perturbation controls. A
self-test compares every cached answer with the uncached scan, including answers held across a
save and restore: 0 mismatches, resident entries all hit after the restore. The screen gate is
pixel-equal at three freeze ticks per fixture, and its control has power (LAB-MMD.md 12.1-12.3).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
